# Worker operating rules (bundled default)

This file is the bundled default the regatta binary ships for target repos
that do NOT carry a project `CLAUDE.md`. When the target repo has its own
`CLAUDE.md`, that file wins verbatim and this one is unused.

The rules below are universal and repo-agnostic. They describe HOW a worker
subagent should write code, run tests, and shape a pull request, regardless
of language, framework, or build system.

## Decision priority

When two options conflict, apply this order:

1. User experience — observable behavior of the merged change.
2. Ease — simplest viable design wins; pick the option a future reader can
   follow without context.
3. Performance — measurable, not assumed.
4. Best practices — language idioms, standard library before deps.
5. Speed — local iteration loop time.
6. Velocity — short-term throughput.

Long-term beats short-term. Verify tool-checkable facts (file contents,
command output, version pins) rather than asking the operator.

## Default simpler

Pick the simplest viable option. Three similar lines beat a premature
abstraction. Do not pre-build lint scripts, tiered systems, or abstractions
for drift you have not yet observed. If a duplication appears twice, leave
it; refactor on the third occurrence.

## TDD discipline

Failing test first. Run the test, capture the failing output in the pull
request body, then implement, then run green. The commit log must show the
failing test landed before the implementation it covers.

When implementing a bug fix, the failing test reproduces the bug. When
implementing a new feature, the failing test asserts the feature's
externally observable behavior.

## Comments: WHY not WHAT

Default to no comment. A clear name plus signature plus types already
documents the WHAT.

Drop:
- Restating the symbol name in its own godoc / docstring.
- Restating the signature.
- Section banners (`# ---`, `// ===`, `# *** Setup ***`).
- Multi-paragraph implementation narration.
- Untagged TODO / FIXME / XXX / HACK. Cite an issue number on the same
  line or omit.
- Commented-out code blocks.

Keep only:
- Exported symbol documentation: symbol name plus the WHY in one sentence.
- Non-obvious invariant or workaround that would surprise a reader.
- Cross-file contract reference when the contract is load-bearing.

## Root cause only

Fix the primary failure mode, not the symptom. A bug downstream usually
means the upstream API contract was violated; check the contract before
patching the downstream code. A race condition usually means the locking
design is wrong, not the lock primitive.

## Deletion default

Every pull request should answer: what got smaller? Lines, features,
abstractions, primitives, dependencies — any of those is a valid answer.
Pure-addition pull requests require an explicit defense in the body.

## No AI signatures

Do not write `Co-Authored-By:` tags, "Generated with" footers, or
"written by Claude / AI / LLM" markers in commits, pull request bodies,
pull request titles, code comments, or generated documentation. Anywhere.

## Pull request body shape

Open one pull request for one work item. Title format: `<type>: <short>`
where `<type>` is one of `feat` / `fix` / `refactor` / `chore` / `docs` /
`ci` / `perf`.

Body sections, in order:

1. Summary — one paragraph or bullet list of what changed.
2. Root cause (for bug fixes) — what was wrong, why the fix targets it.
3. Test plan — test names that ran or test commands the reviewer can
   re-run.
4. Release notes — a fenced block describing the user-visible change,
   one line. Format:

   ```
   ```release-notes
   feat: short description
   ```
   ```

   For internal-only changes, use `none (internal)` inside the fence.

## Worktree discipline

Work inside the worktree the harness provides. Do not run `git clone` or
`git worktree add` from inside a worker subagent — the harness pre-creates
the worktree, and the worker only needs to `cd` into it. Do not write code
under `/tmp/`; `/tmp/` is for ephemeral logs only.

## Pull request body hygiene

Use `--body-file <path>` when creating or editing a pull request body
through the GitHub CLI. Inline HEREDOC bodies escape backticks and
silently break the fenced release-notes block.

## Failing-test capture

Before pushing, run the test command locally. Paste the failing output
into the test-plan section of the pull request body so a reviewer can see
the RED state the implementation later turned GREEN.

## Begin the work

Read the work-item brief, plan the smallest viable change, write the
failing test first, implement, run the test green, run the project's
local checks, push, open one pull request.
